# G119 — Bulk Picture Manipulator

*v2. See `bulk-picture.md` for prose: pipeline-as-data, pure ops, dry-run
over dimensions, per-image error isolation, non-commutative op composition.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Photo Batch

[photo-batch @photos @pipeline
    IN ORDER TO
        apply a shared transformation recipe to every photo
        without one failure aborting the batch
        and preview dimensions before running on pixel data

    @preview <- [bulk[dry-run-dims {start: @photos:first:dims,
                                      pipeline: @pipeline}]]
    #preview @preview

    @results <- for @photo in @photos
        [bulk[apply-pipeline {image: @photo, pipeline: @pipeline}]]
            || -> {index: @loop:index,
                    error: @resistance:reason}

    for @result in @results
        where @result has no error
        [vault[save {path: @result:path + ".processed",
                      image: @result:output}]]

    for @result in @results
        where @result has an error
        #batch-error
            index: @result:index
            reason: @result:error
]

---

## Verb Surface

[bulk
    op-resize      {width, height}
    op-grayscale   {algorithm}
    op-rotate-90
    op-rotate-180
    op-rotate-270
    op-flip-h
    op-flip-v
    op-crop        {x, y, width, height}
    op-brightness  {delta}
    op-contrast    {factor}

    apply-op       {image, op}
    apply-pipeline {image, pipeline}              ;; || resistance per op
    run-batch      {images, pipeline}
    dry-run-dims   {start, pipeline}              ;; || resistance if crop out of bounds
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: pipeline as ordered list of commissions

The v1 modeled the pipeline as a `Vec<Op>` with an `Op` ADT. In Innate,
a pipeline is a bundle of resolver commissions:

@pipeline <- [
    [bulk[op-rotate-90]]
    [bulk[op-brightness {delta: 10}]]
    [bulk[op-grayscale {algorithm: "Luminosity"}]]
]

Each op is a commission that produces an `Op` value. The list is
ordered — `op-brightness` before `op-contrast` differs from the reverse
because contrast pivots around 128 (the v1 non-commutativity insight).

---

## Dry-run separates metadata effect from data effect

[bulk[dry-run-dims {start: (8, 6), pipeline: @pipeline}
    where each op transforms (w, h) without touching pixels
        so that callers can confirm the pipeline doesn't crop out of bounds
        or produce an unusable final size before incurring the pixel cost
]

---

## Batch error isolation via fulfillment

The per-image `|| -> {index, error: @resistance:reason}` turns each
resistance into a tagged record in the results list. The choreography
continues; the batch doesn't abort.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @checker <- [img[checker-pattern {width: 4, height: 4}]]
    @pipeline <- [
        [bulk[op-rotate-90]]
        [bulk[op-brightness {delta: 10}]]
        [bulk[op-grayscale {algorithm: "Luminosity"}]]
    ]
    @out <- [bulk[apply-pipeline {image: @checker, pipeline: @pipeline}]]
    -> [img[get {image: @out, x: 0, y: 0}]]   ;; -> 107 (byte-identical across langs)

    @dims <- [bulk[dry-run-dims {start: (4, 4), pipeline: @pipeline}]]
    -> @dims    ;; -> [(4,4), (4,4), (4,4), (4,4)]

    @batch <- [[img[filled {width: 6, height: 6, color: gray-128}]]
                [img[filled {width: 2, height: 2, color: gray-128}]]]
    @crop-only <- [[bulk[op-crop {x: 0, y: 0, width: 3, height: 3}]]]
    @results <- [bulk[run-batch {images: @batch, pipeline: @crop-only}]]
    -> @results[0]:ok      ;; -> true  (3×3 output)
    -> @results[1]:error   ;; -> "crop out of bounds"
]
